se.\n- Check if the first two digits are the same: return False.\n- Get the two digits, say d1 and d2.\n- Then, for each position i starting from 2, check if the digit at i is equal to d1 if i is even (starting from 0?), or d2 if odd. Wait, maybe it\'s easier to check that each digit alternates between the two. So for index 0: d1, index 1: d2, index 2: d1, index 3: d2, etc.\n\nSo, for each index i in the string:\n\nif i is even (0, 2, 4...), the digit should be d1.\n\nif i is odd (1, 3, 5...), the digit should be d2.\n\nAlternatively, the pattern alternates between the two digits. So, for each position, the digit should be the first digit if the position is even, and the second if odd.\n\nSo, the code would be something like:\n\ndef is_undulating(s):\n    if len(s) < 2:\n        return False\n    d1 = s[0]\n    d2 = s[1]\n    if d1 == d2:\n        return False\n    for i in range(2, len(s)):\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            expected = d1\n        else:\n            expected = d2\n        if s[i] != expected:\n            return False\n    return True\n\nWait, but let\'s test this logic with some examples.\n\nTest case 1: "1212" → should return True. Let\'s see:\n\nd1 is \'1\', d2 is \'2\'. For i=2 (index 2, even), expected is \'1\' → s[2] is \'1\' → ok. i=3 (odd), expected \'2\' → s[3] is \'2\' → ok. So returns True. Correct.\n\nTest case 2: "1221" → indexes 0:1, 1:2, 2:2 (i=2 even → expected 1, but actual 2 → returns False. Correct.\n\nTest case 3: "121" → indexes 0:1, 1:2, 2:1 (i=2 even → expected 1 → correct. So returns True. Correct.\n\nTest case 4: "11" → first two digits same → returns False. Correct.\n\nTest case 5: "123" → first two are 1 and 2. i=2 is 3, which is not equal to d1 (1) → returns False. Correct.\n\nAnother case: "121212" → returns True. Correct.\n\nAnother case: "12121" → returns True. Correct.\n\nWhat about a number like "1212123"? The first three digits are 1,2,1. Then the fourth is 2, fifth 1, sixth 2, seventh 3. At i=6 (index 6, even), 